WEBVTT

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:24.320
Speaker 1: As the 15th century gave way to the 16th century, Africa found itself caught in a new wave of European exploration.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:36.410
Speaker 1: Portuguese ships were the first to sail into the hearts of African kingdoms, and their arrival marked a new chapter in Christianity's journey on the continent.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.330
Speaker 1: But this was no simple mission of evangelism.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.970
Speaker 1: It was complicated by trade, slavery, and colonization.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:56.710
Speaker 1: In 1483, the Portuguese arrived in the Kingdom of Congo, where King Afonso I embraced Christianity and was baptized.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:03.750
Speaker 1: This new religion was welcomed, and for a while, it seemed like the faith might spread across the region.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:12.950
Speaker 1: But the coming of the slave trade, which involved European traders and African rulers, created a major contradiction.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:22.980
Speaker 1: Though Christianity arrived alongside European influence, it also became entangled with the horrors of the Atlantic slave trade.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:30.420
Speaker 1: Priests became complicit in the trade, and the church profited from baptizing enslaved people.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.500
Speaker 1: Christianity's role in this history is complex and challenging to reckon with.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:44.020
Speaker 1: In the 1570s, Catholicism made its mark in the Niger Delta in the Kingdom of Wall.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:55.930
Speaker 1: Here, Christianity was embraced by the local elite, including the son of the Olu of Worry, who was baptized and named Sebastian Domingos.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:04.330
Speaker 1: Yet, like many other parts of Africa, this faith was often blended with traditional African beliefs.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:12.530
Speaker 1: In 1733, despite Christian influence, the kingdom reverted to traditional religious practices.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:19.570
Speaker 1: The Benin kingdom in modern day Nigeria also experienced early Christian influence.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:27.490
Speaker 1: The Oba was baptized in 1516, but the relationship with Christianity was complex.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:39.750
Speaker 1: While some Christian practices were adopted, the kingdom also practiced human sacrifice, reflecting the deep intertwining of old beliefs with the new ones.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.190
Speaker 1: Protestantism made its mark too, especially on the Gold Coast.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:54.150
Speaker 1: Africans, educated in Europe brought Protestantism back with them, but their return was met with cultural challenges.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:03:03.040
Speaker 1: The influence was limited, as these returnees struggled with language barriers and the acceptance of their new faith.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:13.360
Speaker 1: Meanwhile, the transatlantic slave trade created an environment where Christianity was sometimes used to justify human trafficking.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:20.640
Speaker 1: How could a faith that preached equality and love justify the enslavement of millions?

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:27.600
Speaker 1: In Angola, for instance, the church profited from baptizing and branding slaves.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:35.120
Speaker 1: This uncomfortable relationship between the church and the slave trade would linger for centuries.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.160
Speaker 1: The rise of the abolition movement in the 19th century.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.160
Speaker 1: Would eventually become the story of Christianity in Africa.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.600
Speaker 1: But how did this transformation happen?

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.520
Speaker 1: And what role did Africans play in shaping their own religious destiny?

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:02.040
Speaker 1: Lets move forward to explore the surge of missionary activity and the growth of African led churches.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:10.520
Speaker 1: By the 19th century, the Christian faith in Nigeria began to take on new vigour.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:20.600
Speaker 1: While missionaries had first arrived in the Niger Delta in the 16th century, it was during the 1800s that the tide truly Began to turn.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.560
Speaker 1: Missionary societies were formed dedicated to this cause.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:31.880
Speaker 1: In 1975, the London Missionary Society set sail to Southern Africa.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:40.520
Speaker 1: In 1799, the Church Missionary Society brought the gospel to West Africa.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:51.190
Speaker 1: By 1804, the British and Foreign Bible Society was translating the scriptures into African tongues.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.910
Speaker 1: The Methodist Missionary Society wasn't left behind either.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:07.910
Speaker 1: The Church Missionary Society CMS played a crucial role in this period, Sending workers who would plant churches and schools across the region.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:13.030
Speaker 1: But what made this wave of Christianity different from the past?

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.740
Speaker 1: It was more than just missionaries.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:19.580
Speaker 1: It was about African Christians taking charge.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:27.900
Speaker 1: The abolition of the transatlantic slave trade in 1807 gave the movement its wings.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:29.900
Speaker 1: Rescued Africans.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:37.020
Speaker 1: Many Yoruba settled in Sierra Leone, Embraced Christianity and returned home as missionaries.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:46.410
Speaker 1: These were the Saroz among them, A name that still echoes Bishop Samuel Ajayi Crowther.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:54.250
Speaker 1: Samuel Ajayi Crowther, a former slave who became the first African Anglican bishop in Nigeria.

00:05:54.889 --> 00:06:01.210
Speaker 1: His journey from slavery to leadership embodied the resilience of African Christianity.

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:10.660
Speaker 1: Crowther not only translated the Bible into Yoruba, but also established the foundations of Christian education in Nigeria.

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:16.020
Speaker 1: Was his story the beginning of Nigeria's unique Christian identity?

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:21.140
Speaker 1: By the 1840s, the CMS had boots on the ground.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:31.620
Speaker 1: Ajayi Crowther was ordained, preaching the first service in Yoruba Alongside Reverend Henry Townsend, who would go on to start Nigeria's first newspaper.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.710
Speaker 1: Christianity gained its foothold.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:45.030
Speaker 1: The 19th century witnessed the establishment of mission schools, hospitals and churches across the country.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:49.510
Speaker 1: These became centers of learning and transformation.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:53.670
Speaker 1: The faithful were not just being taught about the Bible.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:07:01.840
Speaker 1: They were being equipped to lead their communities, Blending Western education with traditional African values.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:16.800
Speaker 1: The deposing of Oba Kosoko in Lagos and the rise of Akintoye Was partly orchestrated by Christian activists lobbying the British crown to protect commerce and the gospel.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:21.840
Speaker 1: By the 1850s and beyond, denominations sprang up.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:31.750
Speaker 1: Baptists with Thomas Bowen, Presbyterians with Hope Waddell, Catholics under Antonio Theodore and later Joseph Shanahan.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:47.990
Speaker 1: Shanahan's approach using education to reach children and through them parents Saw Catholic numbers in eastern Nigeria grow from 4,000 to over 110,000 by 1932.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:52.080
Speaker 1: In the north, it was a different story.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:57.920
Speaker 1: Islam had spread via Usman Danfodio's jihad of 1804.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.560
Speaker 1: Christianity crept in slowly.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.320
Speaker 1: Ajayi Crowther opened stations in Bebe and Eger.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:19.320
Speaker 1: But it was the Sudan Interior mission, founded in 1918 by Gowan, Kent and Bingham, that laid the groundwork for today's Eckwa, one of the largest churches in northern Nigeria.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:27.560
Speaker 1: By the end of the 19th century Christianity had established a significant foothold in Nigeria.

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:33.960
Speaker 1: Cities like Lagos, Ibadan and Abelkota became Christian hubs.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.720
Speaker 1: And the faith was rapidly spreading inland.

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:47.240
Speaker 1: As the 20th century approached, Nigeria was on the brink of profound political and social changes.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:51.400
Speaker 1: Would Christianity be a unifying force in the country?

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:57.720
Speaker 1: Or would it face challenges in adapting to the dynamics of post colonial Africa?

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:11.730
Speaker 1: The early 20th century saw the flourishing of Christian institutions in Nigeria, with missionaries building schools and hospitals, leaving a lasting imprint on Nigerian society.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:19.650
Speaker 1: Missionary education in particular became a key vehicle for the spread of Christianity.

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:25.570
Speaker 1: But as Nigeria neared independence, Christianity would face new questions.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:32.770
Speaker 1: Nigeria's path to independence in 1960 marked a turning point for the church.

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:40.950
Speaker 1: While the missionaries had laid the groundwork, the emergence of Nigerian leadership brought new challenges and opportunities.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.350
Speaker 1: Would the church continue to grow under African leadership?

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:50.950
Speaker 1: Or would it struggle to maintain its relevance in a rapidly changing society?

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:55.910
Speaker 1: The church in Nigeria was not waiting for a handover.

00:09:56.630 --> 00:10:01.150
Speaker 1: As far back as 1925, something was already stirring.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:09.390
Speaker 1: Josiah Olunowo Oshitelu had founded the Church of the Lord Aladura, meaning the praying people.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:19.390
Speaker 1: Known for their white garments and deep emphasis on healings, visions and fervent prayer, these churches were not European inventions.

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:23.390
Speaker 1: They were authentically African responses to faith.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:33.240
Speaker 1: The Aladura churches, rooted in Anglican and Methodist backgrounds, weren't just precursors, they were pioneers.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:38.440
Speaker 1: In fact, they started the famous 1930 Ayobabalola revival.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:50.040
Speaker 1: By 1937, the story of Pentecostalism in Nigeria took another turn when Pa Sidney Granville Elton arrived in Elisha from England.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:11:02.770
Speaker 1: His mission to raise a generation of African evangelists, His legacy immeasurable, Elton didn't just preach, he built platforms.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:07.970
Speaker 1: Through him, global evangelists found Nigerian soil fertile.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:20.570
Speaker 1: He helped launch the ministry of a young man named Benson Idahosa, the same Idahosa who would later export Nigerian Pentecostalism to the world stage.

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:33.930
Speaker 1: By the 1960s, university campuses became breeding grounds for Pentecostal movements fueled by ministries like Scripture Union, NIFAS and scm.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:48.090
Speaker 1: From these spaces emerged names like David Oyedukwo, who would go on to found Living Faith Church and preach the controversial but captivating message of what many termed the prosperity gospel.
